<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    GonogoKosUplink's OWN contract slice: the eleven wire payload/command-arg
    types that used to live in mod/Sitrep.Contract/KosCommands.cs, KosRun.cs and
    KosTerminal.cs, relocated here per the operator mandate that NO
    uplink-specific wire type may live in core, even for an in-monorepo Uplink
    (see local_docs/design/2026-08-10-uplink-types-out-of-core-plan.md). Sixth
    and last Uplink through the types-out-of-core mechanism, and the one the plan
    sequenced LAST for a reason: three files rather than one, three separate
    channel families in one slice (kos.processors discovery, the per-CPU
    kos.terminal.<coreId> screen downlink, the kos.run.<coreId> RPC), and eight
    of the eleven are inbound-only command args, more than every earlier slice
    put together.

    The plan's inventory said "9+ types". It is eleven, recounted from
    RtConfig.wirePayloadTypes rather than trusted: KosProcessorInfo,
    KosComputeStatus, KosExecArgs, KosReEnableArgs, KosTerminalFrame,
    KosTerminalOpenArgs, KosKeystrokeArgs, KosTerminalResizeArgs,
    KosTerminalCloseArgs, KosRunArgs, KosRunResult. No enum of any kind, so this
    slice needs no enum codegen leg.

    Deliberately its own small project rather than folding into
    GonogoKosUplink.csproj, and the reason is sharpest of all six here.
    GonogoKosUplink.csproj is net48-only and its HintPaths reach into a LIVE KSP
    install for kOS.dll / kOS.Safe.dll / 0Harmony.dll on top of the usual
    UnityEngine / Assembly-CSharp pair; rtcli could not load that assembly at
    all without one. None of these eleven types touches kOS or KSP (they are
    plain DTOs; every kOS type the Uplink needs is reached from the runtime
    project), so the split keeps the codegen input clean and, unlike every
    predecessor, keeps codegen runnable on a machine with no kOS installed.
    This project's dual-target boilerplate stays a straight copy of
    Sitrep.Contract.csproj's.

    GPL-3.0-only, matching GonogoKosUplink.csproj, the same call the SCANsat
    slice made and for a stronger reason. That Uplink's GPL is provisional and
    on hold; this one's is PERMANENT (LICENSING.md's table: kOS is GPL-3.0-only
    and this Uplink compile-time links it, with no arm's-length route
    available). Nothing in THIS project links kOS, so an MIT re-declaration
    would be defensible on its own linkage, but carving a slice of a permanently
    GPL deliverable back out to MIT is a licensing call to make deliberately,
    not as a side effect of a code move. The whole Uplink reads as one licence,
    and it stays a dependency leaf either way, so the obligation propagates
    nowhere. LICENSE beside this file is the same GPLv3 text as
    ../GonogoKosUplink/LICENSE; kOS's attribution stays in
    ../GonogoKosUplink/NOTICE-KOS.txt.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net48</TargetFrameworks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>GPL-3.0-only</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <AssemblyName>GonogoKosUplink.Contract</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Gonogo.KosUplink</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Reinforced.Typings is a compile-time-only codegen dependency: rtcli
       reads the [TsInterface] attributes baked into this assembly's metadata
       to generate this Uplink's own TS SDK slice (see codegen.sh). Must NOT
       flow to net48 (the runtime TFM GonogoKosUplink.csproj bundles): same
       PrivateAssets/Condition/#if NETSTANDARD2_0 discipline as
       Sitrep.Contract.csproj, for the identical reason (a KSP-loaded net48
       assembly must never try to resolve Reinforced.Typings.dll). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Reinforced.Typings" Version="1.6.7" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'" PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="compile;build;native;contentfiles;analyzers;buildtransitive" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Sitrep.Contract only, Private="false": SitrepContractAttribute/
       SitrepTopicAttribute/SitrepUnitAttribute/Units are core's cross-package
       surface, provided by the core mod (GonogoCore) at runtime, never copied
       here. Same pattern GonogoKosUplink.csproj already uses for its own
       Sitrep.Contract reference. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Sitrep.Contract\Sitrep.Contract.csproj">
      <Private>false</Private>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
